% ============================================================================
% roman-numerals — each Roman symbol's value (adj-facts-stdlib, GRADE-SCHOOL).
% ============================================================================
% A grade-school arithmetic fact: the seven letters of the Roman system and the
% number each one stands for — I is one, V is five, X is ten, and so on up to M
% for a thousand. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? roman_numeral_value(x, $V)        % 10
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `roman_numeral_value(symbol, value)` carrying the table's citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% value WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on any
% letter not in the table (e.g. `q`, which is not a Roman numeral).
%
% Because the `value` column is a NUMBER, a recalled symbol-value composes
% straight into arithmetic: to read a whole numeral like XVI you add the recalled
% values 10 + 5 + 1 = 16 (with a subtractive step for forms like IX). That is the
% concrete bridge from grade-school RECALL of the seven symbols to grade-school
% COMPUTE of a numeral's total.
%
% A NOTE ON CASE: ADJ atoms are lowercase, so the `symbol` key is written with
% the lowercase letters `i, v, x, l, c, d, m`. The source (below) writes the
% symbols as the uppercase Latin letters `I, V, X, L, C, D, M`; the value each
% denotes is identical — only the atom spelling is lowercased so it parses as a
% bare identifier.
%
% Provenance: the seven symbol→value pairs are copied from the sentence quoted in
% `source`, taken verbatim from the Wikipedia "Roman numerals" article's lead at
% the `locator`. `trust authoritative` — a citable encyclopedia reference.
% Nothing here is asserted from memory; every value traces to that sentence.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table roman_numeral_value {
    columns symbol, value

    row (i, 1)
    row (v, 5)
    row (x, 10)
    row (l, 50)
    row (c, 100)
    row (d, 500)
    row (m, 1000)

    source "The modern style of Roman numerals uses only seven letters from the Latin alphabet as symbols: I meaning 1, V meaning 5, X meaning 10, L meaning 50, C meaning 100, D meaning 500, and M meaning 1000."
    locator "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_numerals"
    trust authoritative
}
